Absolutely! What you're aiming to do is called a Distributed JMeter Test Execution, which is the right approach when:
You want to simulate high user loads (e.g., 1,000+ users).
Your local machine has limited resources (CPU, RAM).
You already have access to remote machines (like for your Selenium Grid).
This setup works well within GitLab CI/CD too, just like your Selenium Grid — with some additional configuration.
✅ Step-by-Step Guide: Distributed Load Testing with JMeter
We'll break this into 7 key steps, including GitLab runner integration.
🔧 1. Prepare Your Machines (Master + Slaves)
You need:
1 master (controller) machine → kicks off the test.
N slave (worker) machines → generate load.
✅ Requirements for all machines:
Same version of JMeter installed.
Java JDK installed and properly configured (JAVA_HOME).
Machines are network-accessible (ping, open ports).
Same test script and jmx file across all nodes (shared GitLab repo or synced manually).
⚠️ Ensure ports 1099 and 50000+ are open between master and slaves (default RMI ports).
🔧 2. Configure JMeter on Slave Nodes
On each slave machine:
Navigate to:
${JMETER_HOME}/bin/jmeter-server
Run the server with:
./jmeter-server
(On Windows: jmeter-server.bat)
Optional: Edit the jmeter.properties file:
server_port=1099
server.rmi.localport=50000
🔧 3. Configure JMeter on Master Node
On the master machine, open jmeter.properties and set:
remote_hosts=slave1IP,slave2IP
Example:
remote_hosts=192.168.1.101,192.168.1.102
You can also run JMeter in non-GUI mode with distributed flag.
🔧 4. Start Test from Master Node
Run the test remotely using the following command:
./jmeter -n -t test-plan.jmx -R192.168.1.101,192.168.1.102 -l results.jtl
-n: non-GUI mode
-t: path to test plan
-R: comma-separated list of remote hosts
-l: log file
🔧 5. Split Load Across Machines
In your JMX file:
Set Thread Group to 1,000 users.
JMeter will automatically split the load evenly across all slaves (500 users per machine in your case if 2 machines).
To have different load per machine, you need to split the test plan into 2 .jmx files or use custom remote scripts (advanced).
🔧 6. Run JMeter from GitLab CI/CD
Set up a GitLab runner on the master machine (or a CI runner that can SSH to master).
Example .gitlab-ci.yml:
stages:
  - performance-test

performance_test:
  stage: performance-test
  script:
    - echo "Running JMeter distributed test..."
    - ./jmeter -n -t test-plan.jmx -R192.168.1.101,192.168.1.102 -l results.jtl
  tags:
    - performance
You can also add SSH commands to trigger remote jmeter-server startup or build that into your pipeline with Ansible/Shell scripts.
🔧 7. Analyze the Results
Use jmeter -g results.jtl -o report-folder to generate an HTML report.
Copy and archive results.jtl in GitLab CI for analysis.
🧠 Summary of Key Notes
Component	Details
Master	Initiates the test (you run jmeter here)
Slaves	Run jmeter-server and execute requests
Port Requirements	1099 and 50000+ must be open
GitLab Integration	Trigger jmeter from GitLab via CLI
Load Splitting	Automatically divides users across slaves
